\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,hyperref}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Distance layers and square moments}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
We recover the lower bound $n(6,4,6)\ge24$ from Table~4 of
Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}. Here $n(k,4,\lambda)$ denotes the
least order of a simple graph of degree $k$, girth four and exactly
$\lambda$ squares through each edge, or infinity if none exists.
Two elementary counts serve different parts of the argument. Distance
layers force a five-cycle in any small candidate. The second moments
of common-neighbour counts give a stronger necessary condition within
the acquired search with that cycle prescribed.

\begin{lemma}\label{layers}
Let $G$ be a simple $k$-regular graph of order $n$, with $k\ge2$,
no triangle and no five-cycle. Suppose every edge belongs to exactly
$\lambda$ squares. For every vertex there is a positive integer $b$
satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{both}
 b(2k+1+b-n)\le k(k-1),\qquad
 k(k-1)^2\le b(k-1+\lambda).
\end{equation}
In particular, if $k=6$ and $n\le22$, then $\lambda\ge9$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix $x$, put $X=N(x)$, and let $Y$ be the set of vertices at distance
two from $x$. Write $b=|Y|$ and $r=n-1-k-b$. The set $Y$ is
independent. Indeed, if $y,z\in Y$ were adjacent, choose neighbours
$u,v\in X$ of $y,z$, respectively. If $u=v$ there is a triangle;
otherwise $x,u,y,z,v$ is a simple five-cycle. Both are excluded.

Put $c_y=|N(y)\cap X|$ for $y\in Y$. Since $Y$ is independent,
all neighbours of $y$ outside $X$ belong to the $r$ remaining vertices.
Thus $c_y\ge k-r$. Counting walks of length two from $x$ gives
\[
 \sum_{y\in Y}c_y=k(k-1).
\]
This also shows that $b>0$. Both $\sum_y c_y(c_y-1)$ and $k\lambda$
count twice the squares through $x$: the former by their opposite
vertex, the latter by their two incident edges. Hence
\[
 \sum_{y\in Y}c_y^2=k(k-1+\lambda).
\]
Summing $c_y\ge k-r$ proves the first inequality in~\eqref{both}.
Cauchy--Schwarz proves the second. No transitivity or connectivity
assumption is used.

At $k=6$ and $n\le22$, the first inequality implies
$b(b-9)\le30$, so $b\le11$. If $\lambda\le8$, the second would
require $150\le13b\le143$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

We next work in a partial triangle-free graph which is to be completed
to degree six. A vertex is \emph{closed} when all of its eventual
neighbours have already been selected; the other vertices are
\emph{active}. The acquired completion method~\cite{twenty} retains
exact degree deficits and, for each active $y$, a candidate set $T_y$
containing every possible future neighbour: candidates must be active,
have positive deficit, respect the prescribed cycle, and have no
present common neighbour with $y$.

\begin{lemma}\label{moment}
Fix a closed vertex $x$ and write $X=N(x)$, so $|X|=6$. For every
$y\notin X\cup\{x\}$ let $l_y$ be its present number of neighbours
in $X$. If $y$ is closed put $u_y=l_y$; otherwise put
\[
 u_y=l_y+\min\{r_y,|T_y\cap X|\},
\]
where $r_y$ is its degree deficit. Every completion with constant square
count $\lambda$ supplies integers $c_y$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{relaxation}
 l_y\le c_y\le u_y,\qquad \sum_y c_y=30,\qquad
 \sum_y c_y(c_y-1)=6\lambda.
\end{equation}
The least possible value of the last sum subject to the first two
conditions is found by starting at $c_y=l_y$ and taking the
$30-\sum_y l_y$ cheapest increments, where incrementing a coordinate
from $t$ to $t+1$ costs $2t$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Because $x$ is closed, $X$ is its final neighbour set. Every as yet
unselected edge from an active $y$ to $X$ lies in its candidate set
and consumes one unit of its deficit. This proves the interval bounds.
Triangle-freeness makes $X$ independent, so its six vertices each have
five other neighbours, all outside $X\cup\{x\}$. The first sum is
therefore thirty. Counting the squares through $x$ as in
Lemma~\ref{layers} proves the second sum. Here no restriction on
five-cycles is needed.

For coordinate $y$, its available successive increments have costs
$2l_y,2(l_y+1),\ldots,2(u_y-1)$. The globally cheapest increments
respect every coordinate's order, because every predecessor has a
strictly smaller cost. They therefore give a feasible vector whenever
enough increments exist. Any vector using a more expensive increment
while omitting an available cheaper one can exchange them and reduce
its cost. Repeated exchange proves optimality. If the required total
is negative or exceeds the available capacity, there is no feasible
vector. The intervals can admit choices which do not arise from any
graph; allowing these only lowers the minimum and is safe for rejection.
\end{proof}

For implementation, the number of increments of cost $2t$ is
$|\{y:l_y\le t<u_y\}|$. Adding one at position $l_y$ and subtracting
one at $u_y$ in a difference array gives all six counts by cumulative
sums. Levels $t=0,\ldots,5$ suffice. The current moment plus the
cheapest required increments is the exact relaxed minimum. A node is
rejected if its intervals have insufficient capacity or its minimum
exceeds $6\lambda$. With several target values, comparison to six
times the largest target remains a necessary condition. This test
modifies neither the partial graph nor its branching or symmetry rules.

\begin{lemma}\label{finite}
There is no simple triangle-free graph of degree six, on twenty or
twenty-two vertices, containing a five-cycle and having six squares
through every edge.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Acquire the complete five-cycle partition, integer inventory and
completion proof from~\cite{eighteen,twenty}. Relative to a named
cycle, the sizes $a_i,b_i,z$ record vertices with the two cycle
neighbours $v_{i-1},v_{i+1}$, the single neighbour $v_i$, or none.
They satisfy
\[
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}+b_i=6,\qquad
 \sum_i b_i+2z=2n-30,\qquad a_i\ge1,\quad b_i,z\ge0.
\]
The acquired inventory enumerates all solutions, independently checks
them by the positive $a_i$, and then identifies rotations and reflections
of the named cycle. Its existing completion engine is changed only to
allow order twenty-two and to add Lemma~\ref{moment} at the five
original cycle vertices. Its array lengths and unsigned bit masks
support that order. Every valid completion survives the new test by
the lemma, so the acquired completeness proof still applies.

Both ordinary and sanitized builds complete the following inventories,
with identical outputs and node counts in every case. All outputs are
empty for target six.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{r|r|r|r}
Order&Labelled size patterns&Dihedral representatives&Search nodes\\\hline
20&407&55&55\\
22&512&70&27\,496\,438
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
At order twenty every pattern is rejected at its root. At order
twenty-two the exact leaf test still checks every edge for the target
value. The checker requires completion of every indexed case; a deadline
cannot be interpreted as an empty result. Together with the proved
inventory and necessary pruning rules, the completed records establish
the exclusion.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
\[n(6,4,6)\ge24.\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The acquired tree bound gives
$ n\ge12+\lceil2(25-6)/6\rceil=19$.
Counting incidences of edges and squares gives $8\mid6n\lambda$.
At $\lambda=6$ this requires $n$ even. The only remaining candidate
orders below twenty-four are twenty and twenty-two.
Lemma~\ref{layers} forces a five-cycle in either candidate, and
Lemma~\ref{finite} excludes both.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Verification and attribution.}
The lower bound is a prior entry of Table~4 of~\cite{GJ}, physical
page~20. The tree bound and the complete cycle inventory and search
are acquired with their proofs. The two distance-layer inequalities
and the convex moment relaxation are the added derivation; no general
priority claim is made. An independent integer-assignment enumeration
checks the literal moment-minimization loop on $31\,952$ interval
families and $547\,088$ prescribed sums, in ordinary and sanitized builds.
Every one of the $86$ positive outputs of the earlier twenty-vertex
classification remains unchanged when the new bound is enabled.
These controls check the implementation; the general lemmas prove its
mathematical validity. No open-conjecture, whole-original, Lean or
compression-measurement credit is claimed here.

